            <ArticleTitle>Vitamin D supplementation.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">practneurol-2017-001720</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1136/practneurol-2017-001720</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Vitamin D testing and supplementation is of great interest to neurologists and their patients. Recommended nutritional intakes of vitamin D in the UK remain focused on bone health, despite increasing evidence for a role outside this area. Here we discuss how neurologists might approach vitamin D testing and supplementation, focusing on two conditions associated with vitamin D deficiency that have an increased risk of downstream complications resulting from these: multiple sclerosis and epilepsy. We set out a rationale for testing serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D concentrations and discuss our personal practice in terms of supplementation, with evidence where available.</AbstractText>
